.TH SPEAKERTEST 1
.SH NAME
speakertest \- check voice(2), the held-open voice model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B speakertest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Speakertest
checks
.IR voice (2)
and, because
.IR say (1)
is built on it, protects
.I say
from a regression in either the model-loading or the per-utterance protocol
that used to live in
.I say
itself.
It prints
.B PASS
or a
.B FAIL
line per problem.
.PP
It needs the model and a pool big enough to hold it, so it is not part of
.IR runtests (1)'s
default sweep \(em run it the way
.IR say (1)
documents:
.IP
.EX
emu -pmain=512m -pheap=512m ... speakertest
.EE
.SH "WHAT CAN AND CANNOT BE TESTED"
Nothing here can hear, so \(em like
.IR voicetest (1)
and
.IR phontest (1)
\(em this does not claim any utterance sounds right.
Two things can be established and both are.
.SS "A second utterance is fast"
That is the entire reason this module exists rather than
.I say
loading the model for itself each time.
The check is a ratio, not a fixed number of milliseconds: a second call to
.B speak
must take under a quarter of what
.B open
took to load the model.
Speed only means anything relative to the cost it is not paying again \(em on
a slower machine both numbers move together, so a ratio is what survives
moving to different hardware and a fixed threshold would not.
.SS "feedids agrees with speak"
.IR Say (1)'s
lowest-level form, bare phoneme ids typed by hand, calls
.B feedids
directly to show the model's own input rather than have
.B speak
build the same sequence invisibly.
This reconstructs that sequence independently \(em by calling
.B lookup
itself rather than reusing
.BR speak 's
internals \(em and checks that feeding it through
.B feedids
gives an utterance comparable in length to what
.B speak
returns, with real signal in it.
.PP
Not the same bytes: a
.SM VITS
voice is stochastic by design, and this test found that out by trying exact
equality first.
It failed on the first sample by under a thousandth, which is
.IR voice (2)
working as documented, not a bug in either function \(em see its BUGS.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/speakertest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR voice (2),
.IR phontest (1),
.IR voicetest (1)
.SH BUGS
The speed check is a ratio against one machine's model-load time, on one run.
It has not been seen to be flaky, but it has also not been run enough times
on enough machines to promise it never will be.
.PP
It also spot-checks
.B pcmbytes
and a
.B play
to a file, neither of which needs the model, but neither is the point of this
test and both would be caught just as well by any caller that used them
wrong.
